# Optional companion to the uv setup, for when the CUDA SDK or host compiler is not the
# system one -- e.g. no root, or a toolkit too old for the installed GPU. Copy to .env in the
# repository root, adjust the values, and pass it to each command:
#
#     uv run --env-file .env python ./scripts/train.py -c configs/<config>.yaml
#
# uv does not read .env on its own; export UV_ENV_FILE=.env to avoid repeating the flag.
#
# Three things shape this file:
#
#  * uv sets PATH itself (the virtualenv first) and discards any PATH assigned here, so tools
#    are named by absolute path. This is enough: torch invokes $CUDA_HOME/bin/nvcc and passes
#    $CC to it via -ccbin, so neither needs to be on PATH.
#  * ${PWD} is expanded by uv at launch, so paths stay relative to the checkout in the file
#    but reach subprocesses as absolute ones. Run uv from the repository root.
#  * Variables already exported in your shell WIN over this file -- uv does not override
#    them. The README suggests exporting CUDA_PATH and LD_LIBRARY_PATH from .bashrc; if
#    you did that, unset them before relying on the values here, or they are silently
#    ignored and the extensions build against the wrong toolkit. For the same reason a
#    `${LD_LIBRARY_PATH}` self-append cannot work, so this file assigns outright.
#
# The CUDA version here should match the one the torch pin in pyproject.toml was built
# against; mixing majors is unsupported and torch rejects it outright.

CUDA_HOME=${PWD}/.toolchain/cuda-12.8
CUDA_PATH=${PWD}/.toolchain/cuda-12.8
LD_LIBRARY_PATH=${PWD}/.toolchain/cuda-12.8/lib64

# nvcc invokes unversioned `gcc`/`g++`; point these at symlinks if only versioned ones exist.
CC=${PWD}/.toolchain/bin/gcc
CXX=${PWD}/.toolchain/bin/g++
CUDAHOSTCXX=${PWD}/.toolchain/bin/g++

# Build only for the installed GPU rather than every supported architecture. Set this to your
# own compute capability; 8.6 is an example (Ampere, e.g. RTX 30xx).
TORCH_CUDA_ARCH_LIST=8.6
MAX_JOBS=8
